Model-based testing writes down what a system should do as a small model, then generates sequences of actions and checks that the real system and the model agree after each step. It suits anything with history: sessions, retries, lifecycles.

[David Khourshid](/lore/david-khourshid) described the approach with state machines in [Model-Based Testing in React with State Machines](https://css-tricks.com/model-based-testing-in-react-with-state-machines/). XState builds it into `xstate/graph`: `createTestModel` wraps a machine, and `getShortestPaths` turns it into test paths. "Path generation enables model-based testing - generating test cases directly from your state machine." [Graph & Paths](https://stately.ai/docs/graph)

Effect 4's `Arbitrary.array` generates command sequences. Its documentation uses a state machine as the example, and failed sequences shrink by removing irrelevant commands. [Arbitrary.ts](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/blob/effect%404.0.0-rc.117/packages/effect/src/unstable/arbitrary/Arbitrary.ts)

In rat-stack, [the visitor identity model](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/packages/events/test/visitor.model.test.ts) generates visits from three devices, with and without their cookie, across day boundaries. The model says a persistent id survives every visit that keeps its cookie, and a daily id is stable within a day, sets no cookie, and never repeats on a later day. Making the daily hash ignore the day broke the model at once.

Machines in this repo are [lifecycles](/lore/lifecycles-are-machines), so each one is a ready-made test model. See [property-based testing](/lore/property-based-testing) for single-input rules.
